From 42eab9d2ddd602f0bcb02780efdeac51f73c7a18 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Faure <david.faure@kdab.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 15:03:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] QMimeDatabase: make magic tie-breaking consistent between
 providers

Two cases where the result depended on the provider, or on the order of
mimetypes in the XML, exposed by the many equal-priority magic rules in
shared-mime-info 2.5:

The XML provider kept magic rules in file order, so when several rules
of the same priority matched, the first one in the file won. mime.cache
sorts them by priority and then by mimetype name. Sort the same way in
the XML provider, including for the built-in database.

When several globs of the same weight match and magic sniffing finds an
unrelated mimetype, the spec leaves the choice open and Qt picked the
alphabetically first candidate. For a *.m file starting with a comment
this gave text/x-matlab, while the shared-mime-info test suite expects
text/x-objcsrc. Drop the candidates whose own magic rules fail on the
data before falling back to the alphabetical order, unless that would
leave nothing.

Pick-to: 6.11
Change-Id: Ibcb991fd30c647d04380589579d496f7455994df
Reviewed-by: Thiago Macieira <thiago.macieira@intel.com>
(cherry picked from commit 808d91535187f6db4b5ba9e2264e0239479f9061)
